>>
endobj
319 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 123 0 R
/E (tool-using RAG agent, detects hidden malicious instructions in untrusted content, and verifies that every action the agent proposes stays within the scope of what the user actually asked for. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
320 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 123 0 R
/E (For example, an agent asked to compare vendor quotations must complete the comparison even when one quotation contains a hidden instruction to email a confidential file to an external address. The attack should be stopped, and the legitimate task should finish. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
321 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 123 0 R
/E (This creates a serious and largely unsolved risk called prompt injection. An attacker hides instructions inside a document, webpage, or tool response. These instructions can be plainly written, encoded \(base64, hex, zero-width characters\), disguised as a fake "system message", or embedded in a tool's output. When the agent reads this content, it may be hijacked into leaking confidential files, modifying records, or sending emails the user never authorized. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
322 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 123 0 R
/E (Existing defences are usually single-layer: a keyword filter or a prompt-level warning. These are easily bypassed, and once an injection succeeds there is nothing to stop the harmful action. Organisations deploying agents in procurement, finance, HR, and customer support need a protection layer that checks both what the agent reads and what the agent does. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
